1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,000
On March 19, 2003, coalition troops invaded Iraq, aiming for Saddam Hussein and Baghdad

2
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
with all of the force and might of the United States military.

3
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:23,000
The invasion generated a flood of intelligence from satellite imagery, weapons mounted video feeds,

4
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:30,000
computers, broadcast news reports, and telephone and radio calls from troops on the ground.

5
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:36,000
All of it was gathered, sorted and filtered by United States Central Command, or CENTCOM,

6
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:39,000
a modern military command post.

7
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:44,000
CENTCOM isn't the entire U.S. military structure that monitors the world.

8
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:51,000
It is just one part of that structure, the part that monitors the so-called central region.

9
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,700
Other command posts similar to CENTCOM have different responsibilities,

10
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:57,860
including monitoring other regions of the world

11
00:00:57,861 --> 00:01:01,020
or handling specialized military functions.

12
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:07,240
But all modern command posts are at their core, conduits of information.

13
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:11,300
CENTCOM is the premier example of the modern command post

14
00:01:11,301 --> 00:01:15,100
because it is enmeshed in the most hostile region on the planet.

15
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,420
The tools it uses to gather and sort information have to be cutting edge.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:31,600
There is a belt of conflict and turmoil that runs across the center of the globe, through Africa, through the Middle East and Central Asia.

17
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:36,620
And it is from this belt of turmoil that most of the conflict in today's world is happening.

18
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,820
And CENTCOM happens to sit smack dab in the middle of this belt of turmoil.

19
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:47,220
CENTCOM is a joint command center, headed by General John Abizade.

20
00:01:50,566 --> 00:02:00,372
With its main post at MacDill Air Force Base outside Tampa, Florida, and another forward command post in Doha, Qatar, right within its area of responsibility, or AOR,

21
00:02:00,373 --> 00:02:07,520
these two command posts are tethered by communications equipment, and together they monitor events in a 25-country swath.

22
00:02:07,521 --> 00:02:13,740
Although CENTCOM was established more than two decades ago, CENTCOM's forward command

23
00:02:13,741 --> 00:02:19,440
post in Doha, Qatar was constructed in just three months in 2003, after planners felt

24
00:02:19,441 --> 00:02:24,300
it was safe and necessary to have a military presence within the CENTCOM AOR.

25
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,000
You might ask yourself, is that a workable solution?

26
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:34,800
How can a command have real-time operational control over a very complex area of the world,

27
00:02:34,801 --> 00:02:36,480
12,000 miles away?

28
00:02:36,481 --> 00:02:39,360
quite possible due to technology and communications.

29
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:46,380
Bringing such a complex communications network to an area of the world with relatively low-tech infrastructure

30
00:02:46,381 --> 00:02:51,440
presented challenges to those planning CENTCOM's forward command post in late 2002.

31
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:57,480
We loaded the headquarters aboard a ship and deployed it to Qatar.

32
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:02,960
We sent an advanced party here just about ten days before the headquarters arrived,

33
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:09,960
all crated up in its collapsible boxes and they prepared the area inside a big hangar-like

34
00:03:09,961 --> 00:03:16,800
structure, which was nice because it allowed us better environmental control.

35
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:19,591
We were connected through fiber optic connections.

36
00:03:19,592 --> 00:03:21,880
Then we came in, set up the headquarters,

37
00:03:21,881 --> 00:03:27,740
and we were able to link into those connections to give the headquarters the connectivity

38
00:03:27,741 --> 00:03:30,640
it needed to do the warfighting.

39
00:03:30,641 --> 00:03:36,020
Within months, CENTCOM was a joint command, part in Tampa and part in Doha.

40
00:03:36,021 --> 00:03:41,780
The two posts worked together to monitor the AOR, manage the situation in Afghanistan,

41
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:44,560
and conduct the war in Iraq.

42
00:03:44,561 --> 00:03:50,080
We are focused over here on the immediacy of the war effort in Iraq and Afghanistan.

43
00:03:50,081 --> 00:03:57,240
But at the same time, being forward allows us to go out and engage with all of the 25

44
00:03:57,241 --> 00:04:02,000
countries that are in this area of responsibility.

45
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:07,220
During the war, this place was a hum of professionalism.

46
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:12,040
Each desk that you see behind me was very busy gathering information, assessing what

47
00:04:12,041 --> 00:04:20,000
it meant within the context of the ongoing battle, and framing decisions for the commander.

48
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:24,760
Having CENTCOM's forward command thousands of miles away in Qatar, and its base back

49
00:04:24,761 --> 00:04:30,080
at MacDill, Florida, made for some logistical challenges that only the most sophisticated

50
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:32,360
communications tools could overcome.

51
00:04:32,361 --> 00:04:39,560
Technology today allows us to keep everybody pretty much informed on what's going on and

52
00:04:39,561 --> 00:04:40,560
up to speed.

53
00:04:40,561 --> 00:04:43,640
And that's the real challenge of the split operations.

54
00:04:43,641 --> 00:04:47,080
It's keeping everybody on the same sheet of music.

55
00:04:47,081 --> 00:04:50,200
NavSat Bahrain, this is CENTCOM for a comms check.

56
00:04:50,201 --> 00:04:51,200
How do you read?

57
00:04:51,201 --> 00:04:52,200
CENTCOM, this is NavSat Bahrain.

58
00:04:52,201 --> 00:04:54,200
I have you loud and clear.

59
00:04:54,201 --> 00:05:00,200
satellites, telephones, computer screens, video conferences.

60
00:05:00,201 --> 00:05:04,200
A network of the most sophisticated communications technology available

61
00:05:04,201 --> 00:05:10,200
enables command posts to develop intelligence into an overwhelming military advantage.

62
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:15,200
Communications aided by high technology these days is what we call a force multiplier.

63
00:05:15,201 --> 00:05:20,200
It actually enhances the ability of your force to have any effect in warfare.

64
00:05:20,201 --> 00:05:27,200
The purpose of this secure video teleconference is to facilitate command of the Central Command component commanders...

65
00:05:27,201 --> 00:05:31,200
Modern command posts provide three basic types of information.

66
00:05:31,201 --> 00:05:34,200
First is situational awareness.

67
00:05:34,201 --> 00:05:39,200
We'll provide a general understanding of what is happening in our area of responsibility,

68
00:05:39,201 --> 00:05:44,200
or more specifically, an understanding of the insurgency that is ongoing in Iraq

69
00:05:44,201 --> 00:05:48,200
or ongoing Al-Qaeda terrorist activities in Afghanistan.

70
00:05:48,201 --> 00:05:53,200
The second type of information we'll provide is force protection information.

71
00:05:53,201 --> 00:06:00,200
Essentially, we receive information about threats or impending attacks upon our forces in the area of responsibility.

72
00:06:00,201 --> 00:06:07,200
Finally, we provide targeting information, and targeting is key to any application of military force.

73
00:06:07,201 --> 00:06:14,200
Every moment of every day, intelligence information is analyzed at CENTCOM in Doha and in Florida.

74
00:06:14,201 --> 00:06:19,720
Anything that emits a signal, be it friendly or enemy, gets placed on that screen.

75
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:22,660
In the middle screen is a video teleconference.

76
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:30,280
We do those every day, and we collaborate with DIA, CIA, and also our subordinate commands.

77
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:33,980
To your top right is a tool called Top Scene.

78
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:39,000
We manipulate the imagery so that it appears in a three-dimensional environment.

79
00:06:39,001 --> 00:06:45,120
There's a chat session going on with folks in our forward headquarters in Gutter.

80
00:06:45,121 --> 00:06:50,940
You also see between them there's a phone called the Internet phone, IP phone,

81
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:54,140
and it allows secure communication at the top secret level.

82
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:56,800
Again, a good collaborative tool.

83
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,640
Today's command posts are actually communications hubs,

84
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:05,560
technologically unparalleled and able to gather information from anywhere,

85
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,100
Send it to anywhere.

86
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:09,840
And do it securely and in real time.

87
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:16,960
The technology really allows you to get inside the enemy's decision cycle.

88
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:18,820
And that's critical.

89
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:24,280
I mean, that simply means that we know more than he knows about the battlefield and the battle space that's over it.

90
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:26,940
And we know it sooner and quicker.

91
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:29,960
And therefore, we're able to make decisions on that quicker.

92
00:07:29,961 --> 00:07:35,000
So this process needs to happen very quickly if you want it to be effective in warfare.

93
00:07:35,001 --> 00:07:41,060
And one of the major differences between the 1991 Gulf War and the 2003 Gulf War

94
00:07:41,061 --> 00:07:45,000
is we've cut this process from days to hours to minutes.

95
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,860
What you're looking at here is a three-day look at activity inside of Iraq.

96
00:07:50,900 --> 00:07:54,660
It's human reporting as well as other sources, other intelligence disciplines.

97
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,020
And what we try and do is take a look at a specific site.

98
00:07:58,021 --> 00:08:04,460
We drill down into those sites using other sources to try and identify specific locations

99
00:08:04,461 --> 00:08:06,780
in order to target and track those individuals.

100
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:11,000
We hand them off to Special Operations Forces and Convincial Forces inside of Iraq

101
00:08:11,001 --> 00:08:13,080
so they either capture or kill those individuals.

102
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:18,720
Central Command is just one of nine United States unified command posts

103
00:08:18,721 --> 00:08:20,200
that envelop the entire planet.

104
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,000
There are four other regional commands.

105
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,740
European Command monitors Europe, most of Africa, and much of Asia.

106
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:32,020
Pacific Command monitors Southern Asia, Australia, and Antarctica.

107
00:08:32,021 --> 00:08:36,020
Southern Command monitors portions of Central and South America.

108
00:08:36,021 --> 00:08:41,020
Northern Command, the newest, formed in the wake of the September 11th attacks,

109
00:08:41,021 --> 00:08:45,020
oversees North America and is responsible for homeland security.

110
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:51,020
Four other non-regional commands complete the unified command structure,

111
00:08:51,021 --> 00:08:55,020
including Transportation Command, which handles logistics,

112
00:08:55,021 --> 00:08:59,020
the delivery of all military equipment, food, and even soldiers.

113
00:08:59,021 --> 00:09:07,020
Joint Forces Command is in charge of coordinating the various military branches, troops, and military experimentation.

114
00:09:07,021 --> 00:09:16,020
Strategic Command controls military space operations, strategic warning and intelligence assessments, and global strategic planning.

115
00:09:16,021 --> 00:09:21,020
Special Operations Command controls secret military operations requiring specialized training.

116
00:09:21,021 --> 00:09:26,020
Each unified command has a command structure similar to CENTCOM.

117
00:09:26,021 --> 00:09:32,080
Commander CENTCOM answers directly to the Secretary of Defense and he or she to the President.

118
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:39,200
So there's a direct chain of command with only one person in between the President of the United States and the Central Command Commander.

119
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:45,720
And what is true of CENTCOM's communications network is true of each of the command posts among the unified commands.

120
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:53,380
But because CENTCOM's AOR is the hotspot, it frequently implements the latest technology first,

121
00:09:53,381 --> 00:09:59,740
enabling commanders like Avasaid to literally conduct a war from thousands of miles away if they need to.

122
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:07,680
The commander now has the ability to choose his location rather than being dictated by geography.

123
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:15,160
We can provide the commander with all the information that he needs no matter where he's located on the planet.

124
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:20,040
Modern technology has made command posts like CENTCOM possible.

125
00:10:20,041 --> 00:10:24,060
although the long history of warfare is marked by much slower communication.

126
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:29,480
But when information was available, it often gave a decisive advantage.

127
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:34,660
The regions contested by the Palestinians and Israelis

128
00:10:34,661 --> 00:10:39,180
are actually under the jurisdiction of European Command, not Central Command.

129
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:42,160
Command Central will return on Modern Marvels.

130
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:48,540
Military intelligence has always been a vital component of every commander's arsenal.

131
00:10:48,541 --> 00:10:55,340
Today it continues to provide a tactical advantage to command posts like CENTCOM,

132
00:10:56,040 --> 00:11:00,520
just as it has since the very first command post ever gathered information about an enemy.

133
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:05,340
From the very beginning that we have descriptions of battles,

134
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:08,160
that's what we always look for, you know, the intelligence.

135
00:11:08,280 --> 00:11:10,340
Who had it? Who needed it? Did it get there in time?

136
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:13,920
Ancient commanders were also kings,

137
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:17,920
which meant political and military powers were centralized within the leader.

138
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:25,320
Thutmose III, for example, a famous conquering general had dozens of campaigns in the north

139
00:11:25,321 --> 00:11:27,480
and southern border and Woodleuthan himself.

140
00:11:27,481 --> 00:11:32,940
Now, he didn't have to report back to himself because he's already in the field.

141
00:11:32,941 --> 00:11:38,820
But often, word had to be sent from one commander to another, or to his troops, usually over

142
00:11:38,821 --> 00:11:41,380
a battlefield.

143
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:45,400
Sometimes flags and musical instruments were used to convey tactical information over the

144
00:11:45,401 --> 00:11:48,380
din of battle.

145
00:11:48,381 --> 00:11:54,440
signals originating in the recesses of history were adapted and employed well into the 20th century.

146
00:11:55,540 --> 00:12:00,880
Some of the earliest forms of communications included horns, bugles, conch shells, and drums.

147
00:12:01,160 --> 00:12:06,065
The bugle, for instance, was used to give tactical signals on the battlefield.

148
00:12:06,066 --> 00:12:07,260
A certain series of

149
00:12:07,261 --> 00:12:12,860
notes would mean certain things to the troops, and the noise of the bugle could carry above the

150
00:12:12,861 --> 00:12:16,118
in a battle, troops could hear it and react accordingly.

151
00:12:16,119 --> 00:12:17,980
To the actual soldier in combat,

152
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:22,163
the flag was a focal point for him in a chaos of war.

153
00:12:22,164 --> 00:12:24,720
A soldier, bullets flying around him,

154
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:26,251
smoke blinding him, whatever.

155
00:12:26,252 --> 00:12:29,420
If he could see the flag, if he could see the direction the flag was

156
00:12:29,421 --> 00:12:32,988
moving, he could focus on that flag and follow it.

157
00:12:32,989 --> 00:12:36,200
Over greater distances, visual cues were more

158
00:12:36,201 --> 00:12:40,743
difficult. Sometimes simple messages could be relayed using fire.

159
00:12:40,744 --> 00:12:42,560
That type of signal can be

160
00:12:42,561 --> 00:12:44,140
of the one if by land, two if by sea.

161
00:12:44,440 --> 00:12:47,100
It will just tell you that there's been an invasion.

162
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:49,480
It has to be a prearranged signal.

163
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,300
The Romans improved on this simple communication tool

164
00:12:54,301 --> 00:12:56,820
using an A-frame made of large timbers

165
00:12:56,821 --> 00:12:59,000
that could be either raised or lowered.

166
00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:02,740
On each timber was a fire that signaled a code.

167
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:06,420
You break the alphabet up into blocks

168
00:13:06,421 --> 00:13:08,480
of so many letters per block.

169
00:13:08,660 --> 00:13:11,160
The first torch tells you which block.

170
00:13:11,161 --> 00:13:15,140
the second signal tells you which letter within the block.

171
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:18,780
And so you can actually spell things out alphabetically.

172
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:22,980
But most intelligence from ancient battlefields and commanders

173
00:13:22,981 --> 00:13:25,320
required transporting a physical thing,

174
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:30,040
using a messenger to verbally relay the message or hand over the tablet.

175
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:35,540
Technology was basic, a swift runner or a good horse, and a good road.

176
00:13:35,541 --> 00:13:45,100
Sending a messenger or a horse rider through a wilderness can't really count on that, especially during inclement weather.

177
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:54,240
Now a road gives you a path, that way the messenger knows the way, knows the way back, and also during weather it increases the chance of getting there.

178
00:13:55,120 --> 00:14:02,400
Once all roads led to Rome, military strategists could combine this broadcast network with a courier service,

179
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:08,120
by foot or horse, to relay information from the far reaches of the empire back to the Senate.

180
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:13,220
But lag time, sometimes as great as months from an outpost like Britain,

181
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:19,420
prevented distant capitals like Rome from functioning as battle-time command posts in outlying areas.

182
00:14:19,421 --> 00:14:25,200
Centralization was possible to the extent that Rome was the capital.

183
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:33,100
And the Senate obviously ran the war because it sent the consuls out in order to execute that war.

184
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:35,600
Very often foreign policy was made on the borders.

185
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:41,080
At a time when military commanders were among the few who were literate,

186
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:45,460
written communication was a form of security, though it wasn't foolproof.

187
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:50,140
Messages by courier could also be intercepted, with monumental consequences.

188
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:55,780
When Hannibal was in the south of Italy, waiting for his brother to reinforce him,

189
00:14:56,220 --> 00:15:01,740
his brother sends a letter to Hannibal, saying, let's meet up and we'll crush the Romans.

190
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,580
Hannibal writes back, then the Romans intercept it.

191
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:06,820
So now they know where his reinforcements are.

192
00:15:06,821 --> 00:15:11,180
They proceed to the north of Italy, defeat Hasdrubal, cut his head off,

193
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,580
and then ride all the way down to the south of Italy.

194
00:15:14,581 --> 00:15:19,000
and bowl the head into the tent of Hannibal.

195
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,680
Sort of a message, you know, your reinforcements aren't coming.

196
00:15:23,080 --> 00:15:25,860
Securing intelligence sometimes required a disguise,

197
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:28,680
a message scratched on tablets of lead

198
00:15:28,681 --> 00:15:31,340
and tied to a swimmer's arm for waterproof delivery.

199
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:35,380
Or etched on thin sheets of lead,

200
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:38,240
then curled into a tube to be worn as a woman's earring.

201
00:15:40,580 --> 00:15:43,020
Or tattooed on the shaved head of a slave

202
00:15:43,021 --> 00:15:45,820
and delivered only after his hair had grown back.

203
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:49,000
The only message he was to deliver was shave my head.

204
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:52,300
It would work with a military censor now.

205
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:57,580
Military centralization didn't improve in the centuries following the Roman Empire.

206
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:00,940
In fact, it declined during the Middle Ages,

207
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:05,500
as road systems fell apart and feudal hierarchy decentralized power,

208
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:07,440
both political and military.

209
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:12,020
You still had, essentially, communications that was,

210
00:16:12,021 --> 00:16:15,680
for the most part on the battlefield, no faster than your fastest rider.

211
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:19,520
And that was the way of things for some two millennia.

212
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:24,060
But a decisive change was evident during the American Civil War

213
00:16:24,061 --> 00:16:26,700
due to two revolutionary technologies.

214
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:32,140
The first was the telegraph, which for the first time since fire

215
00:16:32,141 --> 00:16:36,240
signals made distant communication possible without transporting a physical

216
00:16:36,241 --> 00:16:36,941
message.

217
00:16:36,942 --> 00:16:41,720
This is an example of a Civil War telegraph key.

218
00:16:41,721 --> 00:16:46,720
This particular example is called a camelback because of the hump of the lever arm for the key.

219
00:16:46,721 --> 00:16:52,720
What you would have here are a couple of contacts that once the contacts are closed,

220
00:16:52,721 --> 00:16:57,720
a spark is created which travels along the line to a sounder.

221
00:16:57,721 --> 00:17:03,720
This would be at a distance station, and the spark goes through these coils here,

222
00:17:03,721 --> 00:17:06,960
through induction makes this lever arm click against the other.

223
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:12,640
Battlefield reports now traveled at electric speed.

224
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:17,080
The dashes and dots of Morse code still needed to be translated,

225
00:17:17,740 --> 00:17:22,020
but distant commanders could coordinate quickly without leaving their command posts.

226
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:27,960
The military telegraph was used to connect general headquarters to corps headquarters,

227
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:32,080
down to division headquarters, but it usually didn't extend much farther than that,

228
00:17:32,081 --> 00:17:36,300
down to the company level, down to the foot soldier, the front lines, if you will.

229
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:39,900
Below division commanders, communication was verbal.

230
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:43,260
General Grant, who had a telegraph key at his command post,

231
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:46,040
could send messages to President Lincoln in Washington

232
00:17:46,041 --> 00:17:49,320
faster than he could send messages to his own lieutenants.

233
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:51,840
But the telegraph presented its own challenges.

234
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:57,440
There are attempts to link division commanders with corps commanders,

235
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:00,340
with army commanders at Gettysburg by running telegraph wire.

236
00:18:00,341 --> 00:18:04,120
And the experiment was partially successful.

237
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:08,140
A skilled telegrapher could send messages quite quickly,

238
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:12,080
so you could get an answer back in 15 or 20 minutes to a query.

239
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:19,040
A command post like Grant's, usually established in a nearby commandeered home or even a church,

240
00:18:19,520 --> 00:18:24,200
might have anywhere from 5 to 15 men, mostly clerks hand-copying orders.

241
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:29,880
Others would gather information from couriers and dispatch it to officers.

242
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:32,260
Still others would man the telegraph.

243
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,740
Tables were used to display maps of nearby battlefields.

244
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:42,660
These maps might even include topographic information gathered by map-making scouts.

245
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:49,000
The second revolutionary technology that changed the Civil War command post was the railroad.

246
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:51,920
The railroad kind of drove the telegraph.

247
00:18:52,020 --> 00:18:55,980
In other words, with the railroad being able to move a large number of troops quickly,

248
00:18:56,120 --> 00:18:59,220
you had to have means to get that word to the commander.

249
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:03,840
A steam locomotive could sustain speeds far exceeding the top speed of a horse,

250
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:05,880
as long as the track was maintained,

251
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:09,240
and the military could transport more on them than just couriers.

252
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:15,860
It was able to utilize the railroad in moving large numbers of troops rapidly to the field.

253
00:19:15,861 --> 00:19:19,820
Jefferson Davis himself, for example, at the first Battle of Manassas,

254
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:24,740
telegraphing the commander on the field to bring reinforcements,

255
00:19:24,860 --> 00:19:26,520
and they arrive by railroad.

256
00:19:26,521 --> 00:19:33,920
Command posts were able to collect remote information, evaluate it, then swiftly act with decisive force.

257
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:38,280
The North controlled more telegraph lines and railroad track than the South.

258
00:19:39,340 --> 00:19:44,240
So communication was a tactical advantage, as long as the post could manage all the intelligence.

259
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:47,640
And that required a staff of command post office workers.

260
00:19:48,860 --> 00:19:54,960
In addition to bureaucracy, centralization created another burden, at least from a military perspective.

261
00:19:54,961 --> 00:19:56,900
Civilian Oversight.

262
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:03,840
These telegraph lines become very important to connecting Army field commanders

263
00:20:03,841 --> 00:20:08,560
to the Central Command in Washington at the War Department.

264
00:20:08,760 --> 00:20:12,100
That's where the telegraph lines all fed into.

265
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:17,700
Curiously enough, they never continued the telegraph lines over to the White House just across the street.

266
00:20:18,180 --> 00:20:21,360
Abraham Lincoln would make his daily journeys from the White House

267
00:20:21,361 --> 00:20:25,900
and sat patiently listening to the ticker machines bring in the reports.

268
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:32,040
As important as the telegraph and the railroad were to the evolution of command posts,

269
00:20:32,580 --> 00:20:36,060
they would both be eclipsed by innovations in communication technology

270
00:20:36,061 --> 00:20:39,620
that took place in the years following the end of the Civil War.

271
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:47,080
Sending messages via carrier pigeons by exploiting their natural homing instincts

272
00:20:47,081 --> 00:20:51,700
has a long military history, but an inconsistent one for the United States.

273
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:53,678
During World War I, U.S.

274
00:20:53,679 --> 00:20:57,300
carrier pigeons made excellent targets for enemy guns in Europe.

275
00:20:58,480 --> 00:21:00,920
Command Central will return on Modern Marvels.

276
00:21:02,380 --> 00:21:08,940
Command posts continued their path toward further centralization in part due to an 1876 invention.

277
00:21:10,540 --> 00:21:13,640
Alexander Graham Bell's telephone improved on the telegraph

278
00:21:13,641 --> 00:21:17,460
by sending a human voice along the cables rather than a coded blip.

279
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:22,880
The phone made real-time two-way conversation possible over great distances.

280
00:21:24,180 --> 00:21:26,680
The military was eager to exploit its potential.

281
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:34,120
By the 1880s, the United States Army is pushing for a field telephone,

282
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:36,480
and the person doing it is Adolphus Greeley.

283
00:21:36,481 --> 00:21:40,420
He develops the field telephone, and the United States Army uses it.

284
00:21:40,421 --> 00:21:43,960
Here you have a camp telephone of World War I vintage.

285
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:47,200
Telephony had developed by the start of the World War,

286
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:51,420
and this was a new method of communication used within the front lines.

287
00:21:51,900 --> 00:21:54,920
You would have two telephone units connected by a wire, of course,

288
00:21:55,160 --> 00:21:58,640
and if you wanted to make a call, you'd lift up on the handset here,

289
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:00,440
reach over here and turn a crank,

290
00:22:01,160 --> 00:22:04,240
which would send a signal down the wire and ring a bell on the other end,

291
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:06,860
alerting the other end that there was a call coming in.

292
00:22:06,861 --> 00:22:10,260
And once you got the call on the other line, you would just talk normally.

293
00:22:10,420 --> 00:22:14,140
A large battery inside the unit provided the power.

294
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:20,060
The field telephone brought communication from command central to just behind the front.

295
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:26,000
But because it needed a wire connection to a network, it was vulnerable to artillery.

296
00:22:28,140 --> 00:22:34,680
The logical improvement over the telegraph and telephone was real-time two-way communication without a landline.

297
00:22:34,681 --> 00:22:40,840
Marconi patented a wireless telegraph in 1895, precursor to the radio.

298
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,280
But early wireless devices were big and heavy.

299
00:22:45,080 --> 00:22:49,160
Marconi's was practicable aboard ships where weight didn't make much difference

300
00:22:49,161 --> 00:22:53,060
and was adopted by the Navy by 1910 or 11.

301
00:22:54,420 --> 00:23:02,100
Ironically, World War I military command centers were able to communicate with naval ships far out at sea with relative ease using the radio.

302
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:06,540
Yet communication with front-line troops in the foxholes of eastern France

303
00:23:06,541 --> 00:23:10,000
was still impossible due to the size of the unwieldy devices.

304
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:14,840
One reason why World War I offensives bogged down

305
00:23:14,841 --> 00:23:20,280
is because the infantry generally tries to advance in concert with the artillery,

306
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:22,580
with the rolling barrages.

307
00:23:27,320 --> 00:23:29,520
But inevitably there were snags.

308
00:23:29,960 --> 00:23:31,920
Barbed wire would slow infantrymen down.

309
00:23:32,100 --> 00:23:39,540
And so the artillery barrage, a time barrage, would roll forward, but the infantry would generally fall behind.

310
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:52,540
More precisely, there was no quick way for the men in the trenches to communicate with the artillery via the command post to challenges they faced at the front.

311
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:55,460
Messengers proved too slow.

312
00:23:56,540 --> 00:24:01,540
But elsewhere in the theater of war, improvements in transportation were aiding military communication.

313
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:09,660
The internal combustion engine paved the way for the automobile, faster than a courier's horse and more agile than a railroad locomotive.

314
00:24:12,800 --> 00:24:19,200
But it was the airplane's use of the internal combustion engine that really provided a leap forward in communication.

315
00:24:21,020 --> 00:24:30,580
The United States Army Signal Corps issues the first contract for an airplane, for a military airplane, with the Wright brothers in 1908.

316
00:24:30,581 --> 00:24:35,580
So this is hardly five years after the Wright brothers first flight.

317
00:24:35,581 --> 00:24:40,580
But make no mistake, in the first World War, the key importance of the aircraft was reconnaissance.

318
00:24:40,581 --> 00:24:43,580
It wasn't bombing, it wasn't the fighter, it was reconnaissance.

319
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:50,580
Reconnaissance planes provided a tactical advantage due to another technology invented in the 19th century.

320
00:24:50,581 --> 00:24:52,580
The photographic camera.

321
00:24:52,581 --> 00:24:58,400
Because observers couldn't remember or sketch the labyrinthine trench system, photographs

322
00:24:58,401 --> 00:25:04,280
were the most important intelligence a World War I plane could capture.

323
00:25:04,281 --> 00:25:08,220
Photos and other data could be couriered by auto or flown back to General Pershing's

324
00:25:08,221 --> 00:25:11,900
headquarters in western France.

325
00:25:11,901 --> 00:25:15,460
Pershing and his staff created a command central out of commandeered French buildings

326
00:25:15,461 --> 00:25:18,220
behind the front.

327
00:25:18,221 --> 00:25:22,380
Couriers gave information from the front to clerks who copied, encoded and sent the

328
00:25:22,381 --> 00:25:26,920
information on through telegraph, telephone or radio connections up the

329
00:25:26,921 --> 00:25:32,200
chain of command to Washington if necessary. Sometimes information from

330
00:25:32,201 --> 00:25:36,500
above came down to Pershing's post where his staff determined who needed to know

331
00:25:36,501 --> 00:25:42,060
what before sending it on its way. Reconnaissance photos, battlefield maps,

332
00:25:42,061 --> 00:25:47,280
intercepted enemy communications, requests for supplies, all passed through

333
00:25:47,281 --> 00:25:51,900
Pershing's command post and were evaluated. The command post had become a

334
00:25:51,901 --> 00:25:56,553
clearinghouse, sorting and prioritizing a deluge of intelligence.

335
00:25:56,554 --> 00:25:58,200
With overseas telegraph

336
00:25:58,201 --> 00:26:03,660
and telephone cables and radio relay stations connecting Pershing to Washington, the United

337
00:26:03,661 --> 00:26:07,800
States was on the verge of running the war thousands of miles from the battlefield.

338
00:26:07,801 --> 00:26:13,100
The Army Signal Corps in World War I connected Washington, D.C., with Pershing's headquarters

339
00:26:13,101 --> 00:26:15,835
in France, with the divisional commanders.

340
00:26:15,836 --> 00:26:18,700
In other words, Washington could talk to the

341
00:26:18,701 --> 00:26:22,320
divisional commanders if Washington so chose to do that.

342
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:24,620
But Washington chose not to.

343
00:26:25,140 --> 00:26:29,860
One reason why Washington wasn't running the war is that Woodrow Wilson did not particularly

344
00:26:29,861 --> 00:26:32,240
feel that he was capable of running the war.

345
00:26:32,241 --> 00:26:37,340
So essentially Washington gave Pershing carte blanche, but certainly this communications

346
00:26:37,341 --> 00:26:42,980
net enabled Pershing to immediately transmit requests for necessities.

347
00:26:43,780 --> 00:26:47,380
Military professionals hoped political leaders would trust the system.

348
00:26:47,381 --> 00:26:53,560
Even after the armistice in 1918, military planners continued improving communications

349
00:26:53,561 --> 00:26:58,540
technology, like the telephone and the radio, strengthening the capabilities of command

350
00:26:58,541 --> 00:27:03,160
posts for the anticipated next war.

351
00:27:03,161 --> 00:27:07,911
And by World War II, new technology was finally able to connect Washington, D.C.

352
00:27:07,912 --> 00:27:08,920
to the commanders

353
00:27:08,921 --> 00:27:11,440
at the front lines.

354
00:27:11,441 --> 00:27:14,480
This is a Signal Corps BC-611 transceiver.

355
00:27:14,481 --> 00:27:18,840
What you would do with this is after you've jumped with it, a lot of times paratroopers

356
00:27:18,841 --> 00:27:24,020
jump with it and they have this cover to keep you from inadvertently sending out a signal,

357
00:27:24,021 --> 00:27:35,600
you take the cover off, you would take off the antenna cover, extend the antenna, and

358
00:27:35,601 --> 00:27:42,380
the act of extending the antenna actually turns the unit on, and then press the PTT

359
00:27:42,381 --> 00:27:45,620
to push to talk switch and talk into this normally.

360
00:27:45,621 --> 00:27:47,360
Release the switch to receive.

361
00:27:48,360 --> 00:27:51,420
You have a sling so you can put it over your shoulder

362
00:27:51,421 --> 00:27:53,760
so you can be looking at a map or whatever

363
00:27:53,761 --> 00:27:55,600
while you're using it or slinging over your back

364
00:27:55,601 --> 00:27:56,620
when not in use.

365
00:27:56,621 --> 00:27:59,200
Turn the unit off, push the antenna back down

366
00:27:59,201 --> 00:28:00,100
into its housing.

367
00:28:04,240 --> 00:28:07,880
This is a BC-1000 walkie-talkie.

368
00:28:07,881 --> 00:28:11,600
This is an FM radio as opposed to the AM transceiver

369
00:28:11,601 --> 00:28:13,600
that we just looked at.

370
00:28:13,601 --> 00:28:16,600
This has greater range than the handheld transceiver,

371
00:28:16,601 --> 00:28:19,200
and it also is less resistant to interference,

372
00:28:19,201 --> 00:28:22,080
thanks to the frequency modulation.

373
00:28:22,081 --> 00:28:26,220
To operate this, you would tune in your frequency here.

374
00:28:26,221 --> 00:28:28,820
Again, this is an advantage over the handheld,

375
00:28:28,821 --> 00:28:30,220
which is a fixed frequency.

376
00:28:30,221 --> 00:28:32,260
This is a tunable frequency.

377
00:28:32,261 --> 00:28:34,960
You have a handset right here.

378
00:28:34,961 --> 00:28:37,420
When you talk, you rotate this little lever

379
00:28:37,421 --> 00:28:40,860
with your thumb to talk, and then release to receive.

380
00:28:41,600 --> 00:28:47,200
The knob switched the circuit from the receiver attached to the earphone to the transmitter

381
00:28:47,201 --> 00:28:50,720
in the mouthpiece.

382
00:28:50,721 --> 00:28:55,360
Secretary of War George Marshall embraced technology, which allowed him to conduct from

383
00:28:55,361 --> 00:29:02,240
Washington a single war in two regions thousands of miles apart, the European Theater and the

384
00:29:02,241 --> 00:29:04,240
Pacific Theater.

385
00:29:04,241 --> 00:29:09,360
This idea of separate theaters of war managed simultaneously from far away was possible

386
00:29:09,361 --> 00:29:11,160
only because of improved communication.

387
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:15,880
Yet Marshall gave his regional commanders enormous autonomy.

388
00:29:16,880 --> 00:29:19,960
If you look at Marshall's administrative philosophy,

389
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:25,520
it's something like that of a very large, well-run corporation with branch offices.

390
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:29,200
And the boss at the center, if he is good,

391
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:34,140
has excellent information flow from the heads of those branch offices.

392
00:29:34,860 --> 00:29:37,200
Eisenhower called the War Department in Washington

393
00:29:37,201 --> 00:29:39,660
from his European theater command post in France,

394
00:29:40,120 --> 00:29:43,240
days after the June 6, 1944 D-Day invasion,

395
00:29:43,700 --> 00:29:45,140
to order vital tank parts.

396
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:48,920
Marshall was also in close communication

397
00:29:48,921 --> 00:29:51,120
with General MacArthur and Admiral Nimitz,

398
00:29:51,540 --> 00:29:52,980
both managing the Pacific Theater.

399
00:29:54,440 --> 00:29:56,140
But on the other side of the front,

400
00:29:56,580 --> 00:29:58,700
there was a perfect illustration of the flaw

401
00:29:58,701 --> 00:30:00,820
inherent in a centralized command structure,

402
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:04,060
a micromanaging dictator at the very center

403
00:30:04,061 --> 00:30:05,620
of a centralized command.

404
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:13,900
Hitler is the perfect example of a national leader who concerned himself not only with the movement of armies,

405
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:20,400
but with the movement of divisions, with the movement of units of brigade size, battalion size,

406
00:30:20,680 --> 00:30:22,880
that is, units of fewer than a thousand men.

407
00:30:23,180 --> 00:30:30,940
And Hitler's interference, which this modern communications made possible, was a great advantage to us.

408
00:30:30,941 --> 00:30:38,440
Communications technology enabled the World War II command post to monitor battlefields across the globe.

409
00:30:38,441 --> 00:30:49,520
But the next war would for the most part be a cold one, based on high-tech spying, greater centralization, and more bureaucracy in the command structure.

410
00:30:51,740 --> 00:31:00,240
As a captain during World War I, future General George Marshall so impressed General Pershing that after the armistice, Pershing made him his aide-de-camp.

411
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:03,740
Command Central will return on Modern Marvels.

412
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:11,960
After World War II, the United States was the dominant Western power against the Communists in the subsequent Cold War.

413
00:31:13,220 --> 00:31:18,860
Former Secretary of War George Marshall worked with President Truman to create a new centralized military

414
00:31:18,861 --> 00:31:23,620
based on regional commands connected by superior communication technology.

415
00:31:24,520 --> 00:31:26,120
His changes began at the top.

416
00:31:26,121 --> 00:31:33,640
He began to back what we would call the Defense Department, that is a single unified cabinet

417
00:31:33,641 --> 00:31:38,680
level department in which all the military branches would be under a single civilian

418
00:31:38,681 --> 00:31:40,740
chief.

419
00:31:40,741 --> 00:31:45,280
Under Truman, Marshall created the Joint Chiefs of Staff, representing the different branches

420
00:31:45,281 --> 00:31:50,560
of the military, which would answer to the newly created Secretary of Defense.

421
00:31:50,561 --> 00:31:53,840
The Secretary of Defense answered only to the President.

422
00:31:53,841 --> 00:31:57,500
Eventually, as the unified command posts we know today evolved,

423
00:31:58,100 --> 00:32:00,860
they too would answer directly to the Secretary of Defense.

424
00:32:01,820 --> 00:32:06,780
So while Marshall does not invent the modern army's organizational structure,

425
00:32:06,781 --> 00:32:12,220
he certainly is the man who was at least as important as anyone else

426
00:32:12,221 --> 00:32:15,360
in showing the way that it should be done.

427
00:32:16,680 --> 00:32:20,080
Technologies that would support and enhance this new command structure

428
00:32:20,081 --> 00:32:22,900
were embodied in the computer and the satellite.

429
00:32:23,840 --> 00:32:36,100
Our need to have rapid communications was, of course, pushed very hard by the threat of a Soviet surprise attack on the United States, a nuclear Pearl Harbor.

430
00:32:36,101 --> 00:32:46,720
A decapitation strike against the leadership in Washington, D.C., by big Soviet ICBMs might well take out the entire National Command Authority.

431
00:32:46,721 --> 00:32:53,720
Satellites offered instant early detection, important in a Soviet ICBM attack that might take only 30 minutes.

432
00:32:53,721 --> 00:33:00,720
We need the ability to look into the Soviet Union and discern what their intentions are.

433
00:33:00,721 --> 00:33:06,720
It was important to get satellites up, and we achieved that by the early 1960s.

434
00:33:06,721 --> 00:33:12,720
Monitoring events throughout the world was easier with a giant eye floating overhead.

435
00:33:12,721 --> 00:33:17,340
floating overhead that could beam information back to Washington and its military command

436
00:33:17,341 --> 00:33:21,119
posts. Now those military posts needed a giant brain.

437
00:33:21,120 --> 00:33:23,900
Early computers were already tabulating

438
00:33:23,901 --> 00:33:27,912
artillery accuracy during the later stages of World War II.

439
00:33:27,913 --> 00:33:30,020
By the 1950s, they were working

440
00:33:30,021 --> 00:33:32,068
to collect and store intelligence.

441
00:33:32,069 --> 00:33:35,080
You need the help of computers to help process all

442
00:33:35,081 --> 00:33:38,475
this data. It's more data than a human can get through in a timely manner.

443
00:33:38,476 --> 00:33:39,176
In the late

444
00:33:39,177 --> 00:33:44,140
1950, the military created ARPA, the Advanced Research Projects Agency, and its job was

445
00:33:44,141 --> 00:33:46,584
to develop new communications technologies.

446
00:33:46,585 --> 00:33:49,540
Well, ARPA scientists fiddled around with computers,

447
00:33:50,600 --> 00:33:55,480
linked them together, and by 1969, they had four nodes connected together in what they

448
00:33:55,481 --> 00:34:01,840
called an ARPANET. Now, from this ARPANET grew more nodes until it evolved into the

449
00:34:01,841 --> 00:34:03,020
Internet that we know it today.

450
00:34:03,560 --> 00:34:07,620
These innovations made communication ever faster and more complete.

451
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:12,660
Photographs could be sent via satellite, as could television images.

452
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:17,160
Messages could be broadcast from one computer to another.

453
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:22,880
The need to physically courier a message was long gone, but weaknesses in the system weren't.

454
00:34:23,360 --> 00:34:32,980
During the war in Vietnam in the 1960s, opponents with fewer resources and less technology exploited them by fighting low-tech guerrilla-style wars.

455
00:34:32,981 --> 00:34:41,760
They have adopted a very fine cell-like system that suppresses the flow of information.

456
00:34:42,500 --> 00:34:48,440
And this makes it difficult for a technologically oriented society like ours to break into that.

457
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:53,160
America continued to pursue a more centralized regional command structure,

458
00:34:53,560 --> 00:34:57,340
even as the Cold War began to thaw in the 1980s and 1990s.

459
00:34:57,800 --> 00:35:02,540
But events in the Near East and North Africa, plus the strategic importance of oil,

460
00:35:02,980 --> 00:35:05,360
refocused attention toward that region of the world.

461
00:35:06,520 --> 00:35:08,200
Before the creation of Central Command,

462
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:10,760
the world was divided up between the European Command,

463
00:35:10,900 --> 00:35:14,260
which stretches far as Pakistan and Afghanistan,

464
00:35:14,680 --> 00:35:17,660
and the Pacific Command, which actually included Pakistan,

465
00:35:18,400 --> 00:35:22,360
and India, and the dividing line between the commands

466
00:35:22,361 --> 00:35:23,800
was at that border.

467
00:35:24,740 --> 00:35:27,320
A long war between Iran and Iraq

468
00:35:27,321 --> 00:35:29,440
followed the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan

469
00:35:29,441 --> 00:35:33,020
and the Iranian hostage crisis, both in 1979.

470
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:37,660
President Carter created the Rapid Deployment Task Force,

471
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:39,920
which monitored these Middle Eastern events

472
00:35:39,921 --> 00:35:42,420
and was on standby to intervene militarily.

473
00:35:43,460 --> 00:35:45,020
Then when Reagan became president,

474
00:35:45,021 --> 00:35:49,220
he decided that the best thing to do with this rather ad hoc unit

475
00:35:49,221 --> 00:35:51,020
called the Rapid Deployment Joint Task Force

476
00:35:51,021 --> 00:35:54,560
was to turn it into a full-fledged unified command

477
00:35:54,561 --> 00:35:58,620
in the same sense as the European command, the Pacific command.

478
00:35:59,440 --> 00:36:02,420
Central Command became a full command in 1983.

479
00:36:04,440 --> 00:36:11,340
CENTCOM's stewardship of the region was put to the test when in 1990, Saddam Hussein's Iraq invaded Kuwait.

480
00:36:12,440 --> 00:36:17,620
Again, the world's eyes were riveted to this volatile region under Central Command's watch.

481
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:22,008
But what really set things into motion was the U.S.

482
00:36:22,009 --> 00:36:25,000
decision to send over half a million troops,

483
00:36:25,001 --> 00:36:29,160
plus the troops of some 30-odd other coalition members

484
00:36:29,161 --> 00:36:34,260
to invade Iraq and Kuwait and take back Kuwait from the Iraqis in 1991.

485
00:36:35,020 --> 00:36:37,460
This put the command on a totally different footing,

486
00:36:37,461 --> 00:36:42,560
and from that level of strategic attention flowed the new technologies,

487
00:36:42,960 --> 00:36:49,380
the innovative military experiences, and other advantages, if you will, over the 1990s.

488
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:54,380
The Gulf War was also the last war in a long line of American military engagements.

489
00:36:54,381 --> 00:36:59,140
before communications technology truly permeated every aspect of warfare.

490
00:36:59,680 --> 00:37:07,040
The Gulf War of 1991 was, in many ways, the Cold War battle that we never fought against the Soviets.

491
00:37:08,040 --> 00:37:13,440
And we got glimpses of the future of an information technology-driven war, but it was not one.

492
00:37:13,960 --> 00:37:17,200
It was a war that was still fought mostly by tank ships and planes.

493
00:37:17,201 --> 00:37:23,140
And so the modern wars that started with the Civil War and ended with the Gulf War,

494
00:37:23,141 --> 00:37:25,760
were really wars that were characterized by industrial mass.

495
00:37:26,860 --> 00:37:31,840
And World War II really being the apogee of that style of warfare.

496
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:34,000
But if you look at wars of the information age,

497
00:37:34,060 --> 00:37:38,600
in World War II, what it took a thousand bombers to destroy,

498
00:37:40,300 --> 00:37:42,920
could be destroyed by a hundred bombers in Vietnam,

499
00:37:45,780 --> 00:37:49,560
could be destroyed by maybe ten bombers in the 1991 Gulf War,

500
00:37:49,561 --> 00:37:57,400
and can now be destroyed by one bomber or even less in the 2003 Iraq War

501
00:37:57,401 --> 00:38:00,380
because of the advances in information technology and communications,

502
00:38:00,820 --> 00:38:02,660
not so much the advances in weaponry.

503
00:38:03,480 --> 00:38:09,880
The war in Iraq that began on March 19, 2003, was a new war for a new era,

504
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:14,480
managed by computer and telephone and video conferences from command posts,

505
00:38:14,960 --> 00:38:17,340
hundreds, even thousands of miles away.

506
00:38:17,341 --> 00:38:23,020
communication technology no longer simply helped relay messages and gather intelligence.

507
00:38:23,540 --> 00:38:28,380
Now it made bombs smarter, tanks deadlier, and the fighting force smaller.

508
00:38:31,100 --> 00:38:36,400
Of the nine unified commands, Strategic Command is responsible for space surveillance

509
00:38:36,401 --> 00:38:39,460
or monitoring man-made objects orbiting Earth.

510
00:38:40,640 --> 00:38:42,800
Command Central will return on Modern Marvels.

511
00:38:42,801 --> 00:38:54,560
The future of the command post is virtual, a command post that exists in the web of electronic communications, rather than a real place of brick and mortar.

512
00:38:55,380 --> 00:39:04,986
In future wars, computers will allow soldiers and battlefield commanders to pull down from this virtual command post their own options, based on their environment,

513
00:39:04,987 --> 00:39:07,520
rather than having a plan thrust upon them.

514
00:39:07,521 --> 00:39:14,940
We will design systems where the user will be able to determine, much like the users of the Internet do, what they want to see.

515
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:19,799
So the broadcaster won't be in charge of what information goes to the user.

516
00:39:19,800 --> 00:39:21,340
The user will be in charge.

517
00:39:22,120 --> 00:39:25,240
In future wars, information will become the primary weapon.

518
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:31,020
In future wars, soldiers may even remain back at the command post, safely removed from the battlefield.

519
00:39:31,021 --> 00:39:41,027
Why send a soldier or a pilot somewhere where you can send an unmanned vehicle that can provide the same sort of information, in fact better information because an

520
00:39:41,028 --> 00:39:51,156
unmanned vehicle not only can have several different kinds of sensory devices built into it, but it only exposes the technology to danger when it gets in trouble and

521
00:39:51,157 --> 00:39:52,200
not a human life.

522
00:39:53,280 --> 00:39:58,240
Technology can now bring a realistic virtual version of the battlefield right to the command post.

523
00:39:58,241 --> 00:40:07,020
examples of this futuristic net-centric warfare were on display during the invasion of iraq in 2003

524
00:40:07,021 --> 00:40:13,160
look at the second gulf war where daily you're faxing large number of things back and everybody

525
00:40:13,161 --> 00:40:20,340
is on instant messaging and everybody's emailing back with regularity that changed the whole

526
00:40:20,341 --> 00:40:26,080
approach to technology in less than a decade i think sometimes we forget that in the first gulf

527
00:40:26,081 --> 00:40:27,560
we didn't even have email.

528
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:32,440
In both Afghanistan and in Iraq recently,

529
00:40:32,980 --> 00:40:35,260
you had the specter of B-2 bombers

530
00:40:35,261 --> 00:40:38,160
running their daily bombing missions from Missouri

531
00:40:38,161 --> 00:40:40,740
to Afghanistan and Iraq and back again,

532
00:40:41,280 --> 00:40:43,600
controlled by headquarters from Florida

533
00:40:43,601 --> 00:40:46,460
and supplied by information,

534
00:40:46,860 --> 00:40:48,500
gathered maybe another continent away

535
00:40:48,501 --> 00:40:49,780
and sent to them in real time.

536
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:54,360
And that network will only get more powerful.

537
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:58,380
Global positioning systems may someday be sewn into every uniform.

538
00:40:59,060 --> 00:41:01,720
Video cameras may someday be installed into every helmet.

539
00:41:02,640 --> 00:41:07,500
Computer programs can generate nearly infinite scenarios for how a conflict might play out.

540
00:41:08,680 --> 00:41:13,220
The technology exists, but the flow of information may become overwhelming.

541
00:41:13,860 --> 00:41:19,500
Even though we have the ability to get things from satellites and to electronically monitor information,

542
00:41:19,740 --> 00:41:24,320
it complicates things because we wind up with more information than we can analyze at any given time.

543
00:41:24,360 --> 00:41:31,020
And there's always a possibility of, if not human error, human sabotage getting into one of those systems.

544
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:33,580
And what if the whole thing crashes?

545
00:41:35,080 --> 00:41:40,500
An astute adversary will go after this advantage, whether it's through computer viruses,

546
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:42,360
or whether it's through electromagnetic weapons,

547
00:41:43,180 --> 00:41:48,440
or whether it's through any means meant to disrupt this big network of communications

548
00:41:48,441 --> 00:41:51,100
that sits like a bubble above American forces.

549
00:41:51,980 --> 00:41:56,920
Another option for adversaries is to completely slip under the radar, so to speak,

550
00:41:57,120 --> 00:42:01,480
to play by rules other than the ones dictated by sophisticated communication systems.

551
00:42:02,180 --> 00:42:06,900
The real key to success in warfare is to get to the future ahead of the next guy.

552
00:42:07,880 --> 00:42:13,760
And on September 11, 2001, for instance, you could say that al-Qaeda arrived at the future before we did.

553
00:42:13,761 --> 00:42:21,440
They brought a new style of warfare to our shores.

554
00:42:26,300 --> 00:42:32,720
Not every challenge faced by the United States can be handled through an email from a command post thousands of miles away.

555
00:42:33,640 --> 00:42:39,360
It took a very low-tech approach to intelligence gathering to find Saddam Hussein.

556
00:42:39,361 --> 00:42:48,874
So, at what point do we listen to our technical instincts, which allow us to draw further and further away from the battlefield, or do we listen to our political

557
00:42:48,875 --> 00:42:56,260
instincts, which require us to have very physical engagements in the sorts of missions that CENTCOM's involved in these days?

558
00:42:58,660 --> 00:43:05,500
Modern wars, now monitored by command posts like CENTCOM, combine both technological and human components.

559
00:43:06,320 --> 00:43:10,320
Even the communications technology utilized by modern commands has to be read,

560
00:43:11,260 --> 00:43:14,080
interpreted, and acted upon by a person.

561
00:43:15,780 --> 00:43:18,820
At the end of the day, war is a human business.

562
00:43:19,440 --> 00:43:22,640
All commanders bring a different style of command.

563
00:43:23,520 --> 00:43:26,700
Some are very, very comfortable handling it through pure technology.

564
00:43:27,580 --> 00:43:30,880
And perhaps have had the training time to understand all that.

565
00:43:31,360 --> 00:43:32,800
Others like a mixture of it.

566
00:43:32,801 --> 00:43:37,860
I like to look a subordinate in the eyes to understand his confidence.

567
00:43:38,060 --> 00:43:39,480
Does he understand my intent?

568
00:43:40,440 --> 00:43:44,160
And then you've always got the technological piece to back it up.

569
00:43:44,820 --> 00:43:48,140
And if the long history of command post is any indication,

570
00:43:49,340 --> 00:43:53,820
that technological component will only get more sophisticated in the future.

